# Scope to the latest request

## Contract

The **latest user message** defines what to implement. Prior turns may contain
analysis, hypotheses, or a menu of options — those are **not** approval to ship
them unless the user re-asks for that option.

## Do not over-scope

- If the user asks for an **optimizer / search** change (e.g. DE basin jump when
  stuck at penalty), change only the search loop. Do **not** also rewrite the
  loss, NaN policy, bucket rules, or data averaging “while you’re there.”
- If the user asks for a **diagnosis**, do not implement fixes unless they say to.
- If an earlier message listed several solutions and the user then picks one,
  implement **only** that one.

## Loss and science semantics are sticky

Do not change without an explicit ask:

- What makes a loss NaN / penalty (including S bucket `<10` → NaN curves)
- Whether sparse buckets are skipped vs fail-closed
- Canonical windows, truncation, nulls, split definitions (see prior-distance rule)

Prefer asking over “helpfully” softening a hard failure mode the user relies on.

## Long threads

Conversation summaries and earlier proposals bias agents toward implementing the
“root cause fix” instead of the narrow latest ask. When in doubt, re-read the
**last user message** and treat anything else as context only.

If that message is still ambiguous across **named journal/CLI options**
(option 1 vs 2, strat vs fixedstim, …), ask — do not guess. See
`.cursor/rules/confirm-journal-options.mdc`.
